# A coloured pencil drawing.
#
# The graphite sketch with the photograph laid back over its own drawing.
# Colour blending takes hue and saturation from the top layer and lightness
# from what is underneath, so the paper stays paper and the pencil strokes
# pick up the subject's own colours.
#
# The sketch underneath is deliberately *softer* than the graphite one. That
# is the whole trick, and it is not obvious: colour blending keeps the
# backdrop's lightness, so a sketch pushed all the way to white paper leaves
# colour nowhere to live — there is no such thing as a saturated green at 98%
# lightness. Less contrast means more midtone, and midtones are what carry
# colour. Turning `contrast` up makes the drawing brighter and the colour
# weaker at the same time.
#
# `colour` is how much of it comes through. Half is a delicate, faded look;
# three quarters reads more like the pencils were pressed harder.
#
# Takes no source: it re-places the file the document was opened from.

# `softness` is a fraction of the long side rather than a count of pixels,
# because this blur is not drawing a line — it is deciding how much midtone
# the sketch keeps, and that has to mean the same thing whatever size the
# picture is. At the 3052x4060 original it works out at the 60 pixels this
# style was originally tuned with.

param softness = 0.0148
param contrast = 0.10
param colour = 0.5

style pencil-sketch blur={max*softness} contrast={contrast} brightness=0.0
place
set name="Colour" blend="Color" opacity={colour}
